N('cathedral-epiphany-sioux-city','Cathedral of the Epiphany','Sioux City','ia','worship','standing',42.50100,-96.40696,'exact','view','walk','','1891–1904',
  'Begun in 1891 as St. Mary\'s Church; the depression of 1893 stopped work, and for about ten years the parish worshipped in the finished basement. When the Diocese of Sioux City was created in 1902 the bishop chose it as his cathedral, completed the upper church and dedicated it in 1904 as the Cathedral of the Epiphany. The twin spires were only added in 1952. A 1961 remodelling stripped the interior; a restoration rededicated in 1998 put back much of its early look.',
  'A German Gothic church whose 61 stained glass windows came from Franz Mayer of Munich and were restored in the late 1980s. The reredos, side altars and Stations of the Cross came from a closed church in Emmetsburg in the 1990s; the baptismal font is by the Sioux City architect William L. Steele. Masses are said in English, Spanish, Vietnamese and Latin; the parish\'s own page could not be opened when this was written, so Mass times were not checked.',
  'A cathedral that spent its first decade as a basement.',
  'Active Catholic cathedral and parish of the Diocese of Sioux City, 1000 Douglas Street. Seen from the street at any time; go in for Mass (times not checked: the parish site could not be opened when this was written). Parking not checked.',
  [W('Cathedral of the Epiphany (Sioux City, Iowa)')],
  brick='yes', hours='?'),
N('badgerow-building-sioux-city','Badgerow Building','Sioux City','ia','civic','standing',42.49386,-96.40224,'exact','view','walk','','1930–1933',
  'A twelve-storey Art Deco office tower of 1930 to 1933, 169 feet tall, designed by the Sioux City firm of K. E. Westerlind and named for a real estate developer who had come to the town in 1876. It was the tallest building in Sioux City for many years and was long its largest and finest office building. It went on the National Register in 1982, and the Iowa architects\' society later counted it among the state\'s most significant buildings of the 20th century.',
  'A terra cotta front of tall vertical piers with bronze trim and geometric ornament, in which a Native American head is repeated. The register article describes a lobby of black Belgian and pink Tennessee marble with terrazzo floors (whether visitors may go in was not checked).',
  '',
  'Privately owned office building at 622 Fourth Street, downtown. Seen from the street; the lobby was not checked. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Badgerow Building'),L('Sioux City Public Museum: Fourth Street walking tour (PDF)','https://www.SiouxCityMuseum.org/s/Walking-tour-4th-Street-2020-update.pdf')],
  brick='?'),
N('battery-building-sioux-city','Battery Building (Simmons Hardware Company Warehouse)','Sioux City','ia','industry','standing',42.49374,-96.40920,'exact','open','walk','','1905–',
  'A six-storey wholesale hardware warehouse that filled a whole block, designed in 1905 by Gordon, Tracy and Swartwout of New York for the Simmons Hardware Company of St. Louis; its building was overseen by Frank B. Gilbreth, the time-and-motion man of "Cheaper by the Dozen". About two million bricks went into it, on concrete piles driven into soft ground, and it had its own rail sidings. It took the name Battery Building after a battery maker leased it in 1944; Bomgaars used it from 1973 to 2005. Since August 2014 it has been the hotel and restaurants of a casino resort, with the new casino beside it.',
  'The brick block with its 123-foot clock tower, planned as a landmark for the riverfront. The ground floor holds the hotel lobby and two restaurants, so the inside can be seen as a guest or diner (interior detail not checked).',
  'Two million bricks for a hardware warehouse, built by the "Cheaper by the Dozen" father.',
  'Privately owned, 323 Water Street (the Hard Rock Hotel & Casino Sioux City). The lobby and restaurants are open to the public as a working hotel; their hours were not checked. Casino parking beside it (rules not checked).',
  [W('Simmons Hardware Company Warehouse'),W('Hard Rock Hotel & Casino Sioux City')],
  brick='yes', hours='?'),
N('sioux-city-city-hall','Sioux City City Hall (old Federal Building)','Sioux City','ia','civic','standing',42.49658,-96.40598,'exact','view','walk','','1893–1897; rebuilt 1995–1997',
  'Built from 1893 to 1897 as Sioux City\'s first federal building and post office, in Richardsonian Romanesque of grey Bedford limestone, with a clock tower added in 1900. When the post office moved to a new building diagonally across the street in 1933 the old one stood empty; the city bought it, and after its own city hall burned in 1944 made it the new city hall in 1948. By 1993 it was settling and the tower leaning, and the city moved out. Rather than lose it, the city took it down stone by stone, kept the tower, and built a new five-storey city hall behind the old south and west walls, reusing the cleaned limestone, carvings and gargoyles; it opened in August 1997.',
  'The 1897 clock tower, with its restored clock, and the old stone fronts on the south and west sides, carved stone and gargoyles included. Behind them the building is new (1990s), with some of the old wainscoting and marble reused inside.',
  'A city hall taken down stone by stone and put back up around its clock tower.',
  'City of Sioux City offices, 405 Sixth Street, downtown. Seen from the street; opening hours of the offices were not checked. Parking not checked.',
  [L('Sioux City Public Museum: City Halls of Sioux City','https://www.siouxcitymuseum.org/history-website/city-halls-of-sioux-city')],
  brick='?'),
N('castle-on-the-hill-sioux-city','Central High School, the Castle on the Hill','Sioux City','ia','civic','standing',42.50351,-96.40285,'exact','view','walk','','1892–1972',
  'The city\'s high school, opened in the spring of 1893 and called Central High School from 1924, built in Gothic and Romanesque Revival to designs by F. S. Allen of Joliet. The north wing of 1912 was meant to be red brick until the women\'s clubs forced a vote to pay for matching stone, which was smoked to match the older sandstone; a fourth floor replaced the hipped roofs in 1930. The school closed in 1972; a preservation group bought it from the district for one dollar in 1976, and it was turned into apartments, which opened in 2003.',
  'A sandstone castle with a central bell tower between stepped-gable pavilions and corner turrets; the brick annex to the south was added to the register listing in 2016. Apartments inside, not open to visitors.',
  'A high school built as a castle, saved for a dollar.',
  'Private apartments (Castle on the Hill Apartments), 1212 Nebraska Street. View from the street only. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Sioux City Central High School and Central Annex'),L('Sioux City Public Museum: Central High School/Castle on the Hill','https://www.siouxcitymuseum.org/history-website/central-high-school-castle-on-the-hill')],
  brick='?'),
N('morningside-college-historic-district','Morningside College Historic District (Lewis Hall)','Sioux City','ia','inst','standing',42.47461,-96.35966,'exact','view','walk','','1890–1953',
  'The campus of Morningside University, founded in 1889 as the University of the Northwest, which failed in the Panic of 1893; the Methodist Episcopal Church took it over as Morningside College in 1894. Charles City College Hall dates from 1890; the second building got no further than its foundation until it was completed in 1900 as Main Hall, now Lewis Hall. Fire gutted Lewis Hall in 1912 and Charles City College Hall in 1914, and both were rebuilt. The district, most of the campus, went on the National Register in 1997.',
  'Lewis Hall, the massive three-storey hall of 1900 in rough quartzite and brick, and Charles City College Hall (1890), home of the Conservatory of Music for decades, with the 1914 building that is now the library, residence halls and the observatory of 1953 among the contributing buildings. The pin is on Lewis Hall. Interiors not checked.',
  '',
  'Morningside University, a private university, on Peters Avenue in the Morningside part of Sioux City. Seen from the campus streets; parking rules for visitors not checked.',
  [W('Morningside University'),L('National Register form: Morningside College Historic District (PDF)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/97000387_text')],
  brick='?'),
N('martin-hotel-sioux-city','Martin Hotel','Sioux City','ia','hotel','standing',42.49452,-96.40469,'exact','view','walk','','1912–',
  'A Chicago-style hotel built in 1912 on the site of the Hotel Mondamin, which burned that January; when it opened in November it was called the largest and most modern hotel in Sioux City. It was one of Eugene Eppley\'s hotels from 1915 and became the Sheraton-Martin in 1956. In the 1980s it was turned into apartments. Its ballroom was restored between 2001 and 2004.',
  'The hotel block at Fourth and Pierce, at the edge of the Fourth Street Historic District. Now apartments; whether the ballroom can be seen was not checked.',
  '',
  'Private apartments (Martin Tower), 410 Pierce Street, downtown. View from the street. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Martin Hotel (Sioux City, Iowa)'),L('Sioux City Public Museum: Fourth Street walking tour (PDF)','https://www.SiouxCityMuseum.org/s/Walking-tour-4th-Street-2020-update.pdf')],
  brick='?'),
N('sioux-city-masonic-temple','Sioux City Masonic Temple','Sioux City','ia','civic','standing',42.49934,-96.40315,'site','view','walk','','1921–1922',
  'A Masonic temple of 1921 to 1922 by the Sioux City architects Beuttler and Arnold, which cost nearly $300,000 to build and almost $200,000 more to furnish. When it went on the National Register in 2004 it was called an excellent, unaltered example of Spanish Colonial Revival, one of only two downtown commercial buildings in that style; the other is the old Carnegie library two blocks south.',
  'A two-and-a-half to three-storey Spanish Colonial Revival front. Who uses it now, and whether it is ever open, was not checked; interior not checked.',
  '',
  'At 820 Nebraska Street, downtown; owner and present use not checked. View from the street. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Sioux City Masonic Temple')],
  brick='?'),
N('orpheum-theatre-sioux-city','Orpheum Theatre','Sioux City','ia','civic','standing',42.49598,-96.40442,'exact','open','walk','','1927–',
  'A vaudeville and movie palace of 1927 by Rapp and Rapp of Chicago for the Orpheum Circuit, with 2,650 seats over three levels and a Wurlitzer organ. It stands on the site of the county\'s 1878 courthouse, razed after the present courthouse opened in 1918. Cut into two small cinemas in 1982, it went dark in 1992; a restoration of nearly $12 million, begun in 1999, reopened it in 2001, and it is the home of the Sioux City Symphony Orchestra.',
  'The restored auditorium with its boxes, carved and gilded detail and hand-painted ceiling; three of the original crystal chandeliers were found intact above a dropped ceiling. You see the inside by going to a show.',
  'A movie palace found under a dropped ceiling, chandeliers and all.',
  'Performing arts centre at 528 Pierce Street, downtown. Open for performances; advance tickets from the box office at the Tyson Events Center, 401 Gordon Drive, as the theatre\'s ticket page says. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Orpheum Theatre (Sioux City, Iowa)'),L('Orpheum Theatre: ticket information','https://orpheumlive.com/events/ticket-information'),L('Sioux City Public Museum: Pierce Street walking tour (PDF)','https://www.SiouxCityMuseum.org/s/Walking-tour-Pierce-Street-2020-update.pdf')],
  brick='?', hours='Show dates only: the theatre box office opens two hours before the show'),
N('sioux-city-free-public-library','Sioux City Free Public Library (Carnegie building)','Sioux City','ia','civic','standing',42.49668,-96.40160,'exact','view','walk','','1911–1913',
  'The city library spent 1892 to 1913 in the old Municipal Building, which also held the city offices and is gone. Andrew Carnegie gave $75,000 in 1911 for a building of its own, designed by Edward L. Tilton of New York (his only building in Iowa) on land given by a resident; it was dedicated in March 1913. The library stayed until 1989, when it moved into a former bank; investors bought the building in 1996 and made it apartments.',
  'A two-storey red brick Renaissance Revival library under a red tile roof, with a stone doorway. Apartments inside, not open to visitors.',
  '',
  'Private apartments, 705 Sixth Street, downtown. View from the street. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Sioux City Free Public Library'),L('Sioux City Public Museum: City Halls of Sioux City','https://www.siouxcitymuseum.org/history-website/city-halls-of-sioux-city')],
  brick='yes'),
N('first-congregational-church-sioux-city','First Congregational Church (former), Sioux City','Sioux City','ia','worship','standing',42.50420,-96.40397,'exact','view','walk','','1916–1918',
  'A Prairie School church by William L. Steele, built in 1916 to 1918 for a Congregational church founded in 1857, after its older, more traditional church burned in 1916. Steele drew on his work with George Grant Elmslie on the Woodbury County Courthouse, and the two are the only Prairie School buildings known to have a dome. The congregation moved in 1966 and sold it about 1968 to the Sioux City Baptist Church, under whose name it went on the National Register in 1979; it is now a Spanish-language Pentecostal church.',
  'Long horizontal lines of Roman brick and crisp square forms, set against a dome and round-headed windows. Service times not checked; interior not checked.',
  'One of two Prairie School buildings anywhere with a dome, both in Sioux City.',
  'Active church (Iglesia Evangelica Pentecostes Principe de Paz), 1301 Nebraska Street. View from the street; service times not checked. Parking not checked.',
  [W('First Congregational Church, Former (Sioux City, Iowa)')],
  brick='?'),
N('sioux-city-railroad-museum','Milwaukee Railroad Shops (Sioux City Railroad Museum)','Sioux City','ia','rail','standing',42.52799,-96.47653,'site','open','walk','','1916–1918',
  'The engine terminal and car shops the Milwaukee Road built in 1916 to 1918, when eight railroads served the city. At their height in the Second World War 560 men overhauled steam locomotives here; after diesels came the railroad tore much of it down from 1954 and cut the roundhouse from 30 stalls to six. Sold in 1981 and left to decay, it was taken over in 1995 by the Siouxland Historical Railroad Association, which made it a museum around the roundhouse. It went on the National Register in 2018. A Big Sioux River flood in June 2024 shut it for ten months; it reopened in May 2025, with repairs still going on.',
  'The six-stall roundhouse and the other surviving shop buildings on a 32-acre site, with locomotives and cars, among them Great Northern steam locomotive 1355. Part indoor, part outdoor.',
  'A steam-era roundhouse saved by volunteers, then nearly lost to a river.',
  'Sioux City Railroad Museum (Siouxland Historical Railroad Association), 3400 Sioux River Road on Iowa Highway 12 north of town. Admission free, leashed pets welcome; closed on major holidays and may close in bad weather, and its page asks visitors to check its announcements first. Parking on the museum grounds (lot not checked on the map).',
  [W('Milwaukee Railroad Shops Historic District'),L('Sioux City Railroad Museum','https://www.siouxcityrailroadmuseum.org'),L('KWIT: the museum\'s flood recovery (January 2026)','https://www.kwit.org/podcast/spm-news/2026-01-22/sioux-city-railroad-museum-gets-1-9m-grant-for-flooding-recovery')],
  brick='?', hours='Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m., Sunday noon to 4 p.m.; closed major holidays'),
